WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:02.340
-: Okay, so if you skipped the last video

00:02.340 --> 00:04.320
because you know hashing functions

00:04.320 --> 00:06.750
the only thing you miss relevant to git is

00:06.750 --> 00:11.670
that Git uses a specific hashing function called SHA one.

00:11.670 --> 00:13.500
Although this is set to change eventually it still

00:13.500 --> 00:17.280
uses SHA-1 it generates 40 digit hexadecimal numbers.

00:17.280 --> 00:19.230
Exactly what we've been seeing

00:19.230 --> 00:21.360
this is the output of the SHA one algorithm.

00:21.360 --> 00:24.390
This is as well, and these are commit hashes, right?

00:24.390 --> 00:26.490
40 digit commit hashes.

00:26.490 --> 00:30.600
But Git actually uses SHA one in many, many other places

00:30.600 --> 00:32.940
not just to generate commit hashes.

00:32.940 --> 00:35.610
If you recall, let me find the slide.

00:35.610 --> 00:37.260
I mentioned that there are four types

00:37.260 --> 00:39.240
of git objects, four things

00:39.240 --> 00:42.630
four types that Git can store, including commits

00:42.630 --> 00:46.290
but then there's also trees and blobs and annotated tags

00:46.290 --> 00:49.260
all four of which are hashed using SHA one.

00:49.260 --> 00:52.032
So we're gonna get to all four of those data types

00:52.032 --> 00:55.260
or object types throughout the, the course of this section.

00:55.260 --> 00:58.890
But I wanna start by just explaining something about Git.

00:58.890 --> 01:01.560
Git is a key value data store

01:01.560 --> 01:04.500
meaning that we can put data into Git, right?

01:04.500 --> 01:06.600
We ask Git to store something for us.

01:06.600 --> 01:09.750
That could be a file, it could be a folder

01:09.750 --> 01:12.450
or it could be a hundred files in folders.

01:12.450 --> 01:15.720
We could even just tell Git to store a piece of text for us

01:15.720 --> 01:18.480
which we really don't do because all the times we

01:18.480 --> 01:21.240
we make texts in a file, but I'm about to show you

01:21.240 --> 01:22.710
we can just give git a piece

01:22.710 --> 01:25.500
of text and have it store that as well.

01:25.500 --> 01:28.050
So we can put all sorts of content into a Git repo.

01:28.050 --> 01:31.470
And Git is going to hand us back a unique key

01:31.470 --> 01:34.380
that we can then later use to retrieve that content.

01:34.380 --> 01:37.770
Like when you go to a, if you ever go to a bar or a club

01:37.770 --> 01:41.130
or a concert venue and there's a coat check, right?

01:41.130 --> 01:44.070
They, they take your coat, they give you a unique key

01:44.070 --> 01:46.800
that corresponds to your coat, and then later on you go back

01:46.800 --> 01:49.800
and say, hey, here's my key, gimme back my coat, please.

01:49.800 --> 01:54.000
So the keys that we get back in git are SHA one check sums

01:54.000 --> 01:57.000
or the output of the SHA one hashing function.

01:57.000 --> 01:57.833
So here's a demo.

01:57.833 --> 02:02.260
Imagine we have a file called App js, and I'm using Git to

02:03.171 --> 02:04.302
track the changes, right?

02:04.302 --> 02:07.290
I've made one version of it and then a second version of it.

02:07.290 --> 02:11.010
And for each version, I've used Git to store that file

02:11.010 --> 02:12.390
and its contents.

02:12.390 --> 02:15.481
Now that's actually somewhat complicated to understand

02:15.481 --> 02:16.950
'cause it does store files and contents separately

02:16.950 --> 02:18.003
but we'll get there.

02:19.219 --> 02:23.460
Anyway, each one actually gets its own unique key.

02:23.460 --> 02:25.110
So you can see the keys here.

02:25.110 --> 02:28.200
Okay, so they, they're the output of SHA one, just

02:28.200 --> 02:33.200
like a commit hash, and they each correspond to some value

02:33.390 --> 02:35.130
some piece of data.

02:35.130 --> 02:37.990
So if I have one of those keys and I tell Git

02:38.883 --> 02:41.490
please give me the content for this key, it's able to

02:41.490 --> 02:43.740
use that and figure out, oh, I know what I stored

02:43.740 --> 02:44.573
under that.

02:45.462 --> 02:46.680
It's V two of app js.

02:46.680 --> 02:47.513
Here you go.

02:48.701 --> 02:51.105
So we've seen a, a basic version of this using

02:51.105 --> 02:52.598
commit hashes, right?

02:52.598 --> 02:54.630
Where we say things like, check out this commit hash.

02:54.630 --> 02:57.840
I want to use, I wanna view the contents of this commit.

02:57.840 --> 02:59.803
And we give, Git that

02:59.803 --> 03:00.636
commit and then it can go

03:00.636 --> 03:02.280
look up corresponding information.

03:02.280 --> 03:03.930
But what we haven't seen is

03:03.930 --> 03:06.720
that Git uses these hashes all over the place.

03:06.720 --> 03:09.270
It's fundamental to how GIT works

03:09.270 --> 03:11.460
but so much of it is hidden behind the scenes.

03:11.460 --> 03:13.890
So in the next few videos, we're gonna walk through this

03:13.890 --> 03:16.887
we're going to see these different data types

03:16.887 --> 03:18.960
and the role that SHA one hashing function plays.

03:18.960 --> 03:21.420
Not just to make commit hashes, but also

03:21.420 --> 03:24.030
for trees and blobs and then also annotated tags.

03:24.030 --> 03:25.920
But they're kind of an afterthought for us.

03:25.920 --> 03:28.440
So let me summarize that hopefully quickly.

03:28.440 --> 03:31.470
Git allows us to store any sort of data in it

03:31.470 --> 03:34.230
and it will hand us back a key for that data.

03:34.230 --> 03:37.620
And that key will be a SHA one hash that we can then use

03:37.620 --> 03:41.310
at a later date to retrieve the information we stored.

03:41.310 --> 03:43.590
So a key value data store

03:43.590 --> 03:46.260
we store stuff in there and it gives us a key.

03:46.260 --> 03:50.190
We can then come back and give it the key to get that value.

03:50.190 --> 03:51.993
So we'll see that in the next video.
